<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Share Our Voice in the Community</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Paraphrase articles and texts for clearer understanding. Communication: Facilitate peer discussions on various topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can paraphrase articles about local government projects using simple language and relevant visuals (e.g., shows an infographic with funding details and project phases and says, "The city council allocated funds to renovate the sports complex.").
            Can facilitate discussions on civic duties by encouraging classmates to express their opinions, using gestures to prompt responses (e.g., uses an open-hand gesture to invite ideas by saying, "What do you think about participating in community projects?").
            Can clarify complex information from civic articles by breaking down details and using key visuals for better understanding (e.g., shows a flowchart of the voting process and says, "The article means that citizens should vote to have a say in town matters.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays an image of a community project (e.g., a new park being built, a neighborhood clean-up, a public meeting) and a headline from a local news article about it (e.g., "Town Council Approves Funds for Downtown Revitalization"). Students work in pairs to brainstorm two ways citizens can `share` their `voice` regarding such `projects`. The teacher asks, "How can citizens get `involved` and give `feedback`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher takes a complex sentence from a civic article (e.g., "The municipal government has initiated a comprehensive program to enhance urban `sustainability` through waste management reforms.") and models paraphrasing it. Teacher says, "This means, in simpler terms, that the `town council` `should` find new ways to manage trash better for a greener `community`. They `must` make `decisions` that help us be more `responsible`." The teacher uses an open-hand gesture to invite student ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I paraphrase, do I use all the same exact words?" (Expected answer: No, I use simpler words.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the goal of paraphrasing to make complex information easier to understand for everyone?" (Expected answer: Yes, especially for our `community`.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are presented with a short online news article (genre: civic news report, register: formal, format: digital text) from a local newspaper website about a specific `municipal government` `project` (e.g., "New `Public Services` `Project` to `Operate` Next Quarter"). The article contains complex sentences, `passive voice`, and `reported speech` typical of B1.2 level texts (e.g., "It `was announced` that funds `were allocated` for the initiative," or "The mayor `said that` citizen `participation` `was essential`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Can clarify complex information from civic articles by breaking down details and using key visuals for better understanding. Communication: Facilitate peer discussions on various topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main `project` or `decision` the `town council` is introducing in this article?" (Students identify the general topic).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match two or three complex sentences from the article with simpler summary statements provided by the teacher (e.g., "The `authorities` are committed to providing enhanced `public services`" matches with "The `council` `wants` to offer better services to `citizens`."). This helps them identify areas needing mediation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice paraphrasing complex sentences using `passive voice`, `reported speech`, and `modals` (`can`, `should`, `must`). Target vocabulary: `town council`, `community`, `voice`, `feedback`, `decisions`, `public services`, `authorities`, `responsibilities`, `projects`, `citizens`, `participation`, `vote`, `express`, `inform`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> The teacher provides 4-5 complex sentences extracted from the article. Students work individually to rewrite each sentence using `passive voice` or `modals` to simplify it. For example: "The `town council` expects `citizens` to `participate` in `public surveys`." becomes "Citizen `participation` `is expected` in `public surveys`." or "Citizens `should participate` in `public surveys`." Students then practice the pronunciation of `/ɪ/` in words like `public`, `civic`, and `community`, and `/e/` in `share`, `serve` as they read their new sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Pair Paraphrasing):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a complex sentence from the article aloud. Their partner `paraphrases` it using sentence starters like "The article means that...", "In simpler words, the `council` `said that`...", or "This `project` `will involve`..." They `must` use `reported speech` where appropriate (e.g., "The article `said that` `decisions` `were made` based on `community feedback`."). They give each other `feedback` on clarity and pronunciation, especially on word stress in `participation` and `responsibility`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Clarifying Key Information):</b> Students form new pairs. Each pair chooses one paragraph from the article and identifies 2-3 key pieces of information (e.g., a specific `project`, a `civic duty`). They then discuss how they would `clarify` and `explain` this information to someone who hasn't read the article. One student asks for opinions (`What do you think are important duties for citizens?`), and the other uses respectful language (`Could you tell us more about your ideas?`) to `share` their viewpoint.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Podcast Segment Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to prepare and present a 2-3 minute segment for their "Town Council Podcast." The segment `must` focus on `clarifying` a specific `function` of the `town council` or a `civic duty` (`elections`, `public services`, `sustainability initiatives`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects one complex statement or policy related to `Our Town Council` (either from the provided article or a similar one).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They `must` collectively `paraphrase` this information into simple, engaging language suitable for a general podcast audience, focusing on making it understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They `should` incorporate at least two sentences using `passive voice` (e.g., "New `decisions` `are made` based on `community feedback`.") and two sentences using `reported speech` (e.g., "The mayor `said that` new policies `would be implemented` soon.") to explain how `decisions` `are made` or what `authorities` have `informed`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They `must` use `modals` (`should`, `must`, `can`) to express `civic responsibilities` (e.g., "Citizens `must vote` to have a `say` in `community decisions`." or "We `can` `contribute` by `attending` `public` meetings.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student in the group acts as a facilitator, using gestures to prompt responses and encouraging `participation` from group members to `express` their `voice` and `share` additional details, demonstrating sociolinguistic competence (e.g., "Thank you for `sharing` your thoughts on our `community`.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their podcast segments to the class.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "Town Council Podcast" segment presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of `paraphrasing` and `explanation` of complex `civic` information. (Mediation skill)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of `passive voice`, `reported speech`, and `modals` (`can`, `should`, `must`) to clarify `responsibilities` and `decisions`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (`town council`, `voice`, `feedback`, `participation`, `citizens`, `public services`, `sustainability`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to `facilitate` discussion or `explain` clearly, using gestures to prompt responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible `pronunciation` of `/ɪ/`, `/e/`, and appropriate word stress in multisyllabic terms (`participation`, `responsibility`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of formal and respectful language in `civic` discussions (e.g., "Thank you for `sharing` your thoughts on our `community`.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short prompts:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about how to make complex information clearer for my `community` is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I `can` now better `facilitate` a discussion by..."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How `can` my ability to `paraphrase` and `clarify` information help me be a more `involved` citizen in my `community` and `share` my `voice` effectively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of `facilitating` discussions or explaining complex topics do I feel I `must` improve, and how `might` I do that?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this `mediation`-focused lesson? Students were able to connect the linguistic structures to the real-world task of informing their `community`. The podcast format motivated them to simplify and `express` themselves clearly. The focus on `pronunciation` for `/ɪ/` and `/e/` was well-received during practice. The use of gestures to `facilitate` discussion was a visible success.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds `are needed` for future lessons? Some students still struggled with consistently using `reported speech` accurately within the `podcast` context; more targeted practice `should be provided`. Perhaps `visuals` for complex civic `projects` could have been integrated earlier to aid understanding. Ensure all groups get equal opportunities to `share` their `voice` during group work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (This is Lesson 5, the final lesson in this sequence.) This lesson culminates the unit by empowering students to apply all learned skills to mediate and `inform` their `community`. The next theme `will build` on these civic engagement skills, possibly focusing on advocacy or problem-solving within local contexts, where the ability to `clarify` and `facilitate` will remain `essential`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>